│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ faster_tree_focused_beam                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The beam search spends most of its runtime evaluating states. `calculate_scores`          │
│                          │ constructs a DSU for nearly every newly visited board, and the current DSU performs three │
│                          │ heap allocations and vector initialization per evaluation. Since the board has at most    │
│                          │ 100 cells, fixed-size arrays eliminate this allocator overhead and permit evaluating      │
│                          │ substantially more beam candidates within the same time budget.                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Additionally, the evaluation currently penalizes every extra connected component almost   │
│                          │ as strongly as it rewards increasing the largest valid tree by one tile. The actual score │
│                          │ only depends on the largest tree, so this can incorrectly prefer states with many small   │
│                          │ connected fragments over states that have already grown the promising tree but leave      │
│                          │ unrelated tiles disconnected. Reducing this penalty makes the beam more directly optimize │
│                          │ the contest objective while retaining a weak preference for globally connected states.    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The saved evaluation time is invested primarily in the difficult larger boards, where the │
│                          │ original beam widths are comparatively narrow and additional diversity is valuable.       │
│ num_applied              │ 3                                                                                         │
