╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ cycle_aware_beam_score                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The current beam evaluation heavily penalizes the number of connected components, but     │
│                          │ does not distinguish a clean forest from a graph containing cycles. This is problematic   │
│                          │ because the final required object is specifically a tree: a connected cyclic component is │
│                          │ not useful for the objective, while a nearly complete forest is often a promising         │
│                          │ intermediate state.                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This edit adds two structural measurements:                                               │
│                          │ - `tree_vertices`: total number of vertices belonging to acyclic connected components.    │
│                          │ - `cyclic_vertices`: total number of vertices trapped in cyclic components.               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The beam score then rewards preserving tiles in acyclic components and strongly penalizes │
│                          │ vertices in cycles. The component-count penalty is reduced substantially: disconnected    │
│                          │ forest components are normal and often necessary during construction, whereas cycles are  │
│                          │ fundamentally harmful. The original largest-tree size remains the dominant signal.        │
│ num_applied              │ 4                                                                                         │
