│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ avoid_repeated_path_reconstruction                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The beam evaluates a very large number of candidates with identical actual scores,        │
│                          │ especially before a full tree is found because the actual score depends only on           │
│                          │ `max_tree_size`. The existing tie-handling reconstructs the complete history path one to  │
│                          │ three times for every such tie, despite only needing to know whether the candidate path   │
│                          │ is shorter.                                                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Every candidate’s total path length is exactly `next_K_total`, while the current best     │
│                          │ path length is already available as `overall_best_path_str.length()`. Therefore,          │
│                          │ reconstruct the history only if the score strictly improves, or if an equal-score         │
│                          │ candidate is provably shorter. This preserves the existing selection semantics while      │
│                          │ substantially reducing string allocations, reverse operations, and history traversal,     │
│                          │ allowing more beam search work within the time limit.                                     │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
