│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ annealed_treewalk                                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ This replaces the wide beam search with a single long-horizon simulated-annealing walk    │
│                          │ specialized for the sliding-puzzle state graph.                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ For every legal slide, the solver evaluates the resulting board immediately. It uses the  │
│                          │ official largest-tree size as the dominant objective, while also using the number of      │
│                          │ graph components and total matched edges as smoother secondary signals. At high           │
│                          │ temperature it deliberately accepts inferior moves to leave local optima; as the search   │
│                          │ progresses it increasingly chooses the strongest neighbor. A bounded recent-state tabu    │
│                          │ set prevents short loops, and periodic reheating restores exploration if progress stalls. │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Unlike the prior implementation, this does not retain, sort, hash, and copy thousands of  │
│                          │ full board states at every depth. This makes each iteration cheap enough to spend nearly  │
│                          │ the whole move budget on an adaptive trajectory, while retaining the best prefix ever     │
│                          │ seen as the output.                                                                       │
